                "text": "I'm trying to find the pattern in which boxes get removed versus kept - it looks like boxes containing a 2 get eliminated, but there are exceptions like the 7 box in train1 that was empty yet still removed. In train3 almost everything gets removed except box 9, so I'm reconsidering whether the rule is about stacking position rather than just content.\n\nI'm testing a \"support\" theory: maybe boxes with 2 are \"broken,\" and any shape resting only on a broken box also falls, while shapes resting on multiple supports survive. Checking coordinates in train1 - box 3 has a 2 and sits below box 7, which touches only box 3, so box 7 falls too, while box 4 and box 8 touch box 3 but also connect elsewhere, so they survive.\n\nI verify this rule holds across train2 and train3 as well - shapes resting solely on removed shapes get removed, while ones with an unbroken support chain remain. Now I'm mapping out the shapes in the test grid to apply the same logic.\n\n4 kept since it overlaps below, so 1 stays kept too; checking 9a against row3's 1 confirms overlap, keeping it; 7 gets removed. Final kept set: 9a, 1, 4, 3, 9b.",
